# Rule: environment and secrets

Read this before you change the env example file, deploy configuration, container files, or the code that reads environment variables. (Claude Code loads it through `.claude/rules/env-and-secrets.md`; the secrets line is also in the always-loaded `AGENTS.md`.)

| # | Rule |
|---|---|
| E1 | One registry. A variable added to or removed from the code is added to or removed from the env registry and the example file in the same PR. |
| E2 | Validate once, at the boundary. Server env is parsed and validated in one module; nothing else reads the environment directly. |
| E3 | No silent fallbacks in production. Dev defaults apply only outside production. A missing secret or URL fails loudly at startup. |
| E4 | Client-visible variables are public. Never put a secret in a variable that is bundled into client code. |
| E5 | Secrets never enter the repo or an agent's context. `.env*` files are git-ignored (except the example), agents may not read `.env` files, CI uses throwaway values, and secrets never go into PRs, issues or logs. |
| E6 | Production credentials live only where they're needed, and only scripts the owner invokes use them. |
| E7 | A secret that was exposed gets rotated. Deleting the text doesn't un-leak it. |

